The conditional operator is the approximable map from the product of the truth domain with two copies of a domain that relates an input neighbourhood to an output neighbourhood when the conditional guard holds. The master relates because the truth component of the product master is the master of the truth domain, the third clause of the guard. For intersection, the same clause on both outputs passes the inclusion to the intersection by the corresponding guard lemma. For monotonicity, a smaller input has smaller components; the zero and one clauses shrink by the lemmas that a truth neighbourhood contained in those singletons equals them, while a master truth component on the larger input leaves the smaller truth component as the master, zero, or one, and each of those yields the corresponding inclusion into the smaller output.
